Note = {22nd Congress of the International Ergonomics Association-IEA, SOUTH
   KOREA, AUG 25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {Hand injuries, particularly carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS), constitute a
   significant portion of occupational pathologies, predominantly affecting
   women and individuals over 50 years old. Although orthotic products are
   an effective and accessible treatment, patient adherence is crucial to
   prevent invasive interventions. This study employs the Design Thinking
   methodology to design a CTS orthosis based on user perception, aiming to
   improve treatment adherence. Through surveys, in-depth interviews, and
   the use of 3D scanning and 3D printing, proposals were developed, and
   orthosis prototypes were generated. The results indicate that aesthetic
   considerations, comfort, and product perception are fundamental for the
   acceptance and use of orthoses, positively impacting patients' health
   and quality of life.},
