   ROBIO 2017)},
Year = {2017},
Pages = {1856-1861},
Note = {IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Biomimetics (ROBIO),
   Macau, PEOPLES R CHINA, DEC 05-08, 2017},
Organization = {IEEE; IEEE Robot \& Automat Soc; City Univ Hong Kong; Beijing Inst
   Technol; Univ Hong Kong; Univ Macau; Shenzhen Acad Robot},
Abstract = {An affordance-based method is a biology-inspired way to promote the
   cognitive capabilities of robots. Affordances encode the relationships
   between a robot and environment in terms of actions that the robot is
   able to perform. The most notable feature of affordance-based perception
   is that an object is perceived by its functional attributes (e.g.,
   reachable, graspable, etc) instead of its visual attributes (e.g., size,
   shape, etc). Most existing works treat affordance prediction as a binary
   classification problem and assume that an affordance never hides.
   However, sometimes the robot needs to adapt itself, i.e., rotate its
   wrist or turn left, to trigger the hidden affordance because the
   interaction point is very important and the object might be placed in
   any state. In this paper, we consider affordance triggering for
   arbitrary states as a regression problem. In the experiment, a
   manipulative robot could rotate its wrist to find the graspable
   affordance regardless the state of an object.},
