   Consulting Ltd; UN, Environm Planning Bur; Int Soc Indoor Air Qual \&
   Climate; Int Council Res \& Innovat Building \& Construct; Int Initiat
   Sustainable Built Environm; Chinese Assoc Sci \& Technol; States Adm
   Environm Protect; Chinese Assoc Building Decorat; Chinese Soc Forestry;
   Shanton Maofa Telecom Real Estate Dev \& Management Co Ltd; Shenzhen
   McQuay Air Conditioning Co Ltd},
Abstract = {This study investigated the current status of indoor air quality (IAQ)
   in Taiwan district and examined the performance of market available air
   cleaners. Field measurements of indoor air quality at residential homes,
   office buildings, and public areas were conducted in four major
   metropolitan cities in Taiwan, including Taipei, Taichung, Tainan, and
   Kaohsiung. Portable IAQ monitors and detectors generally situated at the
   breathing height in the geometrical center of indoor environments were
   applied for the measurement of CO, SO2, NO/NOx, PM10, radon (Rn), and
   VOCs (i.e. benzene, toluene, ethylene, xylene, formaldehyde, and etc.).
   Field measurements indicated that Taipei demonstrated the worse,
   Kaohsiung and Taichung ranked the second, and Tainan had the best indoor
   air quality in Taiwan's metropolitan cities. Locations that were close
   to main traffic streets and/or nearby industrial areas generally
   demonstrated relatively worse indoor air quality than their neighboring
   environments. In this study, a questionnaire was also undertaken for
   general publics in order to clarify the living styles of citizen. The
   results showed that, on average, people living in urban areas spent
   approximately 92\% of their lifetime in indoor environments such as
   homes, working places, restaurants, automobiles, entertainment
   facilities, and etc. Moreover, in order to further understand the
   performance of consumer preferable air cleaners, ten of them randomly
   purchased from the market were examined in an exposure chamber to test
   their overall removal efficiencies of PM2.5 and VOCs. General speaking,
   the performance of tested air cleaners was not as good as, and some even
   much worse than, they claimed in the advertisements.},
