Note = {13th International Conference on Environmental Science and Technology
   (CEST), Athens, GREECE, SEP 05-07, 2013},
Organization = {Univ Aegean; Global Network Environm Sci \& Technol},
Abstract = {The common perception is that large scale infrastructure projects can
   only damage the environment even though very strict environmental
   requirements are imposed by the Authorities. This was not the case in
   Eleona Attikis region in Greece where a METRO facility was eventually
   built. During the construction phase, hazardous solid waste materials
   were found buried in the urban area where the METRO facility (station,
   tunnel and the depot) is located, bringing up serious environmental
   issues for the natural environment of the region as well as for the
   public health. The protection and restoration of the environment in an
   uncontrolled waste dumpsite require accurate estimation of the extent
   and the intensity of the pollution in the subsurface.
   In the present analysis, an attempt to map the contamination in the
   aquifer of Eleona Attikis where the METRO facility (station, tunnel and
   depot) was eventually built is presented using numerical modelling
   approaches. The results have shown that the removal of the solid waste
   in order to construct the METRO station and the depot was crucial for
   the elimination of the negative impacts in the soil and the subsurface
   water resources of the region. The findings of this analysis proved that
   the implementation of a large transport infrastructure project like the
   construction of the underground METRO facility in Eleona Attikis Greece
   could act environmentally beneficial for the region.},
